1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:05,400
This is a simulation. An approved S3 policy request leaves the backend.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:07,295
But the reply disappears.

3
00:00:07,945 --> 00:00:10,320
Did the change fail? Or just the reply?

4
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:14,645
Before the call, the durable ledger already records CALLING.

5
00:00:14,965 --> 00:00:19,540
AWSOps keeps that uncertainty. It does not blindly send another write.

6
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:23,485
It reconciles the same run, using read-only provider checks.

7
00:00:23,805 --> 00:00:25,793
Here, the observed policy matches.

8
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:29,754
But a matching policy is not fresh Config compliance.

9
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:33,113
Execution stays UNKNOWN. Compliance stays UNKNOWN.

10
00:00:33,432 --> 00:00:35,257
Freshness stays NOT_VERIFIED.

11
00:00:35,578 --> 00:00:37,128
The outcome: NEEDS_REVIEW.

12
00:00:37,778 --> 00:00:43,090
The missing evidence stays visible. This demonstrates control logic, not live acceptance.
